FOREST PRINCIPLES

These are not philosophical principles.

They are working rules.

If a future file in Dark Forest breaks one of these rules, the file should have a very good reason.

---

01 — Observation comes before explanation

Write down what was actually seen before explaining what it means.

A plan shows two spaces connected by a narrow passage.

That is an observation.

"The architect intended psychological compression."

That is an interpretation.

Do not merge them.

The gap between those two sentences is where a lot of bad architectural reasoning begins.

---

02 — An unknown is a real state

"Unknown" is not a temporary embarrassment.

It is a legitimate result.

If we do not know why something happened, write:

unknown

not:

probably because...

unless the probability itself has a reason behind it.

---

03 — Never manufacture a reason for a design decision

If a column count was chosen intuitively, say that.

If a geometry was adjusted because it looked better, say that.

If a proportion came from a source, record the source.

Do not discover the "real logic" of a decision five months later just because the project now needs a clean explanation.

That kind of explanation is not research.

It is reconstruction.

---

04 — Every interpretation can be attacked

An interpretation is not protected because it is elegant.

For every important interpretation, ask:

«What else could explain the same thing?»

The alternative does not have to be better.

It only has to be possible enough to deserve consideration.

---

05 — Contradictions stay visible

When two pieces of information disagree, do not immediately select the convenient one.

Record both.

Record where they disagree.

Record why one might eventually win.

Sometimes the contradiction itself is the useful result.

---

06 — A pattern is not a rule

Seeing the same shape three times does not prove a rule.

Seeing the same relationship across three buildings does not prove intentionality.

A pattern can be:

- intentional
- inherited
- accidental
- produced by constraints
- produced by our method of looking

The Forest keeps these possibilities open.

---

07 — Historical similarity is not historical causation

Two buildings can share a structural idea without one copying the other.

A similar form does not prove transmission.

A date establishes sequence.

It does not establish influence.

When discussing historical relationships, keep:

precedence

similarity

possible influence

documented transmission

separate.

---

08 — The stranger the claim, the stronger the trail

A small observation needs a reasonable record.

A claim that could significantly change MERIDIAN needs a much stronger one.

The Forest should become more demanding as the consequence of a claim increases.

---

09 — Do not reward complexity

A simple explanation that survives testing is better than a beautiful complicated explanation that cannot be tested.

Complexity is allowed.

It is not a virtue by itself.

---

10 — Keep alternatives alive, but not forever

An alternative deserves time when it teaches us something.

It does not deserve infinite storage simply because it is possible.

Every active branch should eventually answer:

«Why are we still following this?»

---

11 — Failure is material

A failed experiment is not an embarrassing footnote.

Record:

- what was attempted
- what was expected
- what happened
- what failed
- what was learned
- what remains uncertain

A failure without a record gets repeated.

---

12 — Do not confuse a useful idea with a true idea

An idea can be useful without being historically true.

A speculative structural analogy can inspire a design investigation without becoming a historical claim.

A cognitive hypothesis can suggest a spatial experiment without becoming neuroscience.

Keep usefulness and truth as separate questions.

---

13 — Separate fields before connecting them

Mathematics should not be used to make architecture sound scientific.

Neuroscience should not be used to decorate a spatial argument.

Physics should not be cited simply because the building contains forces.

First understand what the external field actually says.

Then ask whether the connection to architecture survives.

---

14 — The user is allowed to be wrong

A student entering MERIDIAN may have an interpretation that is completely wrong.

That is fine.

The Forest should not punish the first idea.

It should make it possible to discover that it is wrong.

This is more valuable than forcing everyone to arrive with correct language.

---

15 — The Forest is allowed to surprise the author

If everything that comes out of the Forest was already expected, the Forest is not doing enough.

Unexpected connections, failures, and contradictions are not defects.

They are signals that the system is producing information the author did not explicitly put into it.

---

16 — Nothing becomes important because it has a folder

A folder does not create value.

A connection does.

A test does.

A contradiction does.

A useful question does.

A changed decision does.

The structure exists to support the work, not replace it.

---

17 — Preserve provenance

Every meaningful object should have enough information to recover:

origin → observation → interpretation → change → current state

The exact format can vary.

The trail cannot.

---

18 — Keep dead things readable

When something is terminated, do not rewrite it to make it look stupid.

Record it honestly.

A future reader should be able to understand why it seemed reasonable at the time.

This is how the Forest preserves intelligence rather than only preserving conclusions.

---

19 — Do not clean the history too much

The polished version is useful.

The path that produced it is often more useful.

Where a decision changed because of a mistake, disagreement, failed test, or new evidence, preserve that change.

---

20 — Build for someone who was not here

A future student should be able to enter an old branch without needing the author's memory.

A future researcher should be able to challenge an old claim.

A future designer should be able to use an old insight without inheriting its mistakes.

That is the standard.

---

The shortest version

When unsure, ask five questions:

1. What do we actually know?
2. What are we assuming?
3. What else could explain it?
4. What would change our mind?
5. What could this become?

If a piece of work cannot survive these questions yet, that does not mean it should disappear.

It means it belongs in the Forest.
